Local-resolver changes to BIND 9.20 (used only for the named service; see
pkgs.bind-resolver in flake.nix).

- No network: when no address family has a route, fetches wait for a routing
  change (woken by the routing socket) instead of failing at once, and a
  recursive query that still fails is dropped rather than answered SERVFAIL,
  so clients retry instead of caching the failure.
  Only a "no route" failure counts: a connection that is closed, refused
  or reset proves a route exists and clears the no-network state, which
  otherwise outlived the outage whenever no server answered afterwards
  (every SERVFAIL was then dropped and fetches kept waiting).
  "No network" also needs the kernel to agree: before treating the failures
  as an outage, a UDP connect() (a route lookup that sends nothing) checks
  for a route to a.root-servers.net. A firewall or proxy on the way can fail
  connections with "host unreachable" while the network is up; that is then
  reported as SERVFAIL instead of being waited out in silence.
- stale-answer-ttl may be 0, so stale answers aren't cached by clients.
- Answering from the cache restarts the idle timer of open TCP connections to
  the servers the query would otherwise have gone to (tcp-reuse-timeout), so
  they stay open for reuse; useful when connections are expensive (Tor).
- prefetch-percent N: also refresh a cached RRset once it is N% through its
  original TTL, answering from the cache meanwhile.
- TCP connections to servers are only reused while idle, never shared by
  queries in flight at the same time (pipelined). Some servers reset a
  connection that receives a second query before answering the first
  (Route 53, which serves every CloudFront distribution, does), and that
  fails every query on it. With all recursion over TCP (Tor), concurrent
  A/AAAA/HTTPS lookups for one name then reliably SERVFAILed.
- resolver-tcp-allowance N: the extra time (ms; upstream's fixed 1 s by
  default) a query over TCP may take beyond the server's estimated RTT.
  Through Tor's transparent proxy the connection to the server is made
  only after the query is sent, so a new connection's first answer takes
  the whole stream (and maybe circuit) setup. Servers made TCP-only by
  "server { tcp-only yes; }" now get the allowance too: upstream applies
  that setting after computing the timeout, so their queries got the UDP
  timeout (800 ms for a server not yet measured), shorter than Tor ever
  takes to connect a stream.
- A TCP connection on which a query timed out before any answer ever
  arrived is closed, and one whose queries were all abandoned unanswered is
  not kept for reuse. Through Tor's transparent proxy the connection is
  accepted locally whether or not the server can be reached; kept open (for
  up to tcp-reuse-timeout), Tor went on trying to reach the dead server for
  its full 120 s, spoiling a circuit every CircuitStreamTimeout, and with a
  few dead servers at once every other query waited for circuits.

